<h2>The timeline that framed the dispute</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>26 February 2020.</strong> CIRP initiated against the corporate debtor by the NCLT.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>27 November 2020.</strong> The resolution plan of a third-party applicant approved by 99.22% of the Committee of Creditors.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>25 August 2021.</strong> Before the NCLT could pronounce, the ED provisionally attached immovable property of the debtor in Gautam Buddh Nagar, UP.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>29 September 2021.</strong> NCLT approves the plan under Section 31 IBC.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2022&ndash;2025.</strong> Adjudicating Authority confirms the attachment; the Appellate Tribunal dismisses the challenge in August 2025, reasoning that Section 32A does not reach attachments made before plan approval.</div>
</div>

<h2>The Division Bench&rsquo;s reasoning</h2>
<p>The court&rsquo;s analysis proceeds in three steps. First, the pre-/post-approval distinction drawn by the Tribunal, in the court&rsquo;s assessment at para 32, is &ldquo;an artificial distinction&rdquo; finding &ldquo;no support either in the plain language of Section 32A&rdquo; or in the Supreme Court&rsquo;s judgment upholding the provision. Second, the trigger for Section 32A is the approval of the resolution plan: once control passes to a qualifying third party, that party takes the debtor&rsquo;s properties free from encumbrances, including a PMLA attachment. Third, this reading is not retrospective — it does not annul the attachment as originally made, but operates prospectively from approval to release the property.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>From the judgment (para 36):</strong> once the resolution plan is approved under Section 31, the attachment order under Section 5 of the PMLA &ldquo;can no longer subsist against the property of the corporate debtor&rdquo;, provided the person taking control satisfies Section 32A&rsquo;s conditions.</p>
</div>
<p>Decisive, too, was what the ED did not argue: it was not contended that the resolution applicant was a promoter, a related party or an abettor, that the property fell outside the plan, or that control had not genuinely changed. With no statutory exception invoked, the Tribunal&rsquo;s insistence on a Special Court finding before release was held unsustainable, and its order was set aside.</p>

<h2>Why the ruling matters</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>For resolution applicants.</strong> The clean-slate principle now has clear Delhi Division Bench backing against pre-approval PMLA attachments. Due diligence should still verify that the acquirer is outside Section 32A&rsquo;s exceptions — the protection is conditional, not automatic.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For lenders and the CoC.</strong> Attachments that land mid-CIRP need not depress plan value to zero: the property travels with the plan if the applicant qualifies. The sequencing of CoC approval, attachment and NCLT approval does not change that outcome.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For enforcement proceedings.</strong> The ED&rsquo;s remedies against the individuals responsible remain untouched — Section 32A shields the corporate debtor&rsquo;s property in the hands of a clean acquirer, not the wrongdoers themselves.</div>
</div>
<p>The appeal was allowed with no order as to costs, and the Rs 3.01 crore fixed deposit furnished earlier to secure the title deeds was directed to be released within two weeks of the judgment.</p>
